<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>About spousalsupportcalc.com</h1>
  <p>spousalsupportcalc.com is a free family-law resource maintained by The Click Lab. Our spousal support calculator and guides help separating spouses understand how alimony is calculated, what state-specific formulas apply, what judicial discretion factors affect the outcome, and what practical steps to take when support is contested.</p>

  <h2>What we do</h2>
  <p>We translate family-law concepts — state formula variations, duration guidelines, modification standards, tax treatment changes — into plain-English calculators and explanations that people navigating a divorce can actually use. Spousal support law varies dramatically across states: a 15-year marriage with the same income gap can produce very different outcomes in California, Texas, Florida, or New York. Our content maps these variations and explains the factors that drive them, reviewed by our <a href="/editorial">editorial team</a> with citations to applicable statutes and court rules.</p>
  <p>Our calculator applies the most widely referenced formula approaches as starting estimates and adjusts for state-specific rules where formulas exist. It is designed to help users understand the order of magnitude of a potential support obligation and what factors matter most — not to predict what a judge will order in any specific case.</p>

  <h2>What we don&rsquo;t do</h2>
  <p>We don&rsquo;t provide legal advice. Divorce and spousal support disputes depend heavily on jurisdiction, the specific facts of the marriage, each party&rsquo;s financial situation, the judge assigned to the case, and the procedural posture of the litigation. Formula outputs are starting points; final outcomes result from negotiation, mediation, or judicial determination. For your specific situation, consult a family-law attorney in your state who knows how local courts approach the discretionary factors.</p>

  <h2>How we make money</h2>
  <p>Display advertising through Google AdSense. We don&rsquo;t sell reader data, accept paid placements, or maintain recommended-attorney lists. Our editorial decisions are independent of our advertising relationships. See our <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a> for details.</p>

  <h2>Our commitment to accuracy</h2>
  <p>Spousal support law changes: Florida abolished permanent alimony in 2023; New Jersey created presumptions tied to retirement age; the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act changed federal tax treatment for post-2018 divorces. We monitor these changes and update our content when the law shifts. If you find a formula that has changed, a statute that has been amended, or any factual inaccuracy, please <a href="/contact">contact us</a>.</p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>See our <a href="/contact">contact page</a>.</p>
</BaseLayout>
